Dialogue: 0,0:00:00.00,0:00:02.40,Chapter,,0,0,0,,The Danube\N376 CE
Dialogue: 0,0:00:02.40,0:00:07.00,Narration,,0,0,0,,They looked across the water and\Nbelieved Rome would shelter them.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:07.00,0:00:12.00,Narration,,0,0,0,,Weapons were surrendered for a promise\Nof land and peace.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:12.00,0:00:18.00,Narration,,0,0,0,,The barrier closed. Fields and granaries\Nstayed visible beyond the fence.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:18.00,0:00:25.00,Narration,,0,0,0,,Food became leverage. Bowls stayed empty\Nwhile grain was guarded.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:25.00,0:00:31.00,Narration,,0,0,0,,A bronze horse clasp changed hands for a\Ncrust of bread.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:31.00,0:00:39.00,Narration,,0,0,0,,A brother was taken. The family broke in\Nplain sight.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:39.00,0:00:46.00,Narration,,0,0,0,,She fed what was left of the child. A\Ntorn scarf stayed in his fist.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:46.00,0:00:51.00,Narration,,0,0,0,,Lupicinus offered a banquet — warmth\Nperformed as reconciliation.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:51.00,0:00:53.40,Chapter,,0,0,0,,Marcianople\NThe banquet trap
Dialogue: 0,0:00:53.40,0:00:56.00,Narration,,0,0,0,,Below the hall, Roman blades were\Nreadied in secret.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:56.00,0:01:01.00,Narration,,0,0,0,,The invitation was an execution dressed\Nas hospitality.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:01.00,0:01:07.00,Narration,,0,0,0,,Fritigern took Lupicinus hostage before\Nthe trap could close.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:07.00,0:01:14.00,Narration,,0,0,0,,The hall became violence.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:14.00,0:01:18.00,Narration,,0,0,0,,While spectacle held the room, the\Ncounteroperation moved elsewhere.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:18.00,0:01:24.00,Narration,,0,0,0,,Confiscated arms crossed the postern\Ngate in wagons.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:24.00,0:01:30.00,Narration,,0,0,0,,Cloaked riders carried the weapons into\Nindigo night.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:30.00,0:01:35.00,Narration,,0,0,0,,Fritigern left with Lupicinus still\Nalive.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:35.00,0:01:37.40,Chapter,,0,0,0,,Nine miles from the city\NThey mistook hunger for helplessness
Dialogue: 0,0:01:37.40,0:01:40.00,Narration,,0,0,0,,The stolen weapons reached a wooded\Nclearing.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:40.00,0:01:46.00,Narration,,0,0,0,,Children, wounded, and elders waited\Namong the wagons.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:46.00,0:01:54.00,Narration,,0,0,0,,Travel cloaks fell. Men trained to fight\Nstood in their place.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:54.00,0:02:00.00,Narration,,0,0,0,,Roman infantry approached as if\Nslaughtering refugees would be simple.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:00.00,0:02:05.00,Narration,,0,0,0,,A young soldier saw what was forming\Nahead — and understood too late.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:05.00,0:02:11.00,Narration,,0,0,0,,Arrows found Roman shields.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:11.00,0:02:17.00,Narration,,0,0,0,,Gothic shields drove the line backward.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:17.00,0:02:22.00,Narration,,0,0,0,,Orders dissolved into noise.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:22.00,0:02:27.00,Narration,,0,0,0,,An older Roman saw children beneath the\Ncanvas.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:27.00,0:02:33.00,Narration,,0,0,0,,Alaric watched without understanding\Neverything — only that it was terror.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:33.00,0:02:40.00,Narration,,0,0,0,,The Roman formation fractured. The\NGothic line held.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:40.00,0:02:47.00,Narration,,0,0,0,,Fritigern and Lupicinus fought toward\Nthe wagons where families hid.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:47.00,0:02:57.00,Narration,,0,0,0,,He had taken up the knife for his child.\NHe would not teach the child to worship
Dialogue: 0,0:02:57.00,0:03:03.00,Narration,,0,0,0,,Sunlight and flowers continued after the\Nviolence fell away.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:03.00,0:03:11.00,Narration,,0,0,0,,A damaged Roman helmet passed into the\Nchild's hands — inheritance, not trophy.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:11.00,0:03:16.00,Disclosure,,0,0,0,,Inspired by the Gothic migration and war\Nof 376–382 CE. Some characters,